          { q: "Sliding Window technique — when and how to use it", a: "It is used to solve array or string problems involving contiguous subarrays or substrings. Instead of nested loops, a window (two pointers) slides over the data, expanding or shrinking based on specific conditions. This reduces time complexity from O(n^2) to O(n).", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "Two Pointer technique — common patterns", a: "Common patterns include opposite-directional pointers (e.g., reversing a string, checking palindromes, finding pairs in sorted arrays) and same-directional pointers (e.g., fast and slow pointers for detecting linked list cycles). It is highly effective for reducing time and space complexity.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "Binary Search on answer — explain with an example", a: "Instead of searching for an element, this pattern searches for the optimal answer within a monotonic search space (where conditions flip from invalid to valid). For example, finding the minimum capacity of a ship to transport packages within D days. You binary search the capacity range, checking if each midpoint capacity is feasible.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "Monotonic Stack — what is it and what problems does it solve?", a: "A monotonic stack maintains its elements in a strictly increasing or decreasing order. It is primarily used to find the 'Next Greater Element' or 'Next Smaller Element' in an array in O(n) time. As you iterate, you pop elements that violate the monotonic property, processing them against the current element.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "Topological Sort — when to use it and how does it work?", a: "It is used for scheduling tasks with dependencies, represented as a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) (e.g., course prerequisites). It orders vertices such that for every directed edge u -> v, u comes before v. It can be implemented using Kahn's algorithm (indegrees) or DFS with a visited stack.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union) — explain with path compression and union by rank", a: "Union-Find tracks disjoint sets and efficiently checks if elements belong to the same set. Path compression flattens the tree during the Find operation, pointing nodes directly to the root. Union by rank attaches the smaller tree under the taller tree, keeping it shallow and ensuring nearly O(1) amortized time complexity.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "Segment Tree vs BIT (Binary Indexed Tree / Fenwick Tree)", a: "Both answer range queries and handle point updates in O(log n) time. Segment Trees are more versatile, handling complex associative queries like range minimum or range GCD, and support lazy propagation for range updates. BIT is easier to code, uses less memory, but is typically restricted to prefix sums and inverse operations.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "Backtracking — explain the pattern with N-Queens example", a: "Backtracking builds candidates for a solution incrementally and abandons (backtracks) a candidate immediately if it determines it cannot yield a valid solution. In N-Queens, we place a queen row by row. If placing a queen leads to an attacked position, we undo the placement and try the next column, systematically exploring the state space.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "Greedy algorithms — when to use greedy vs DP?", a: "Greedy algorithms make locally optimal choices at each step, hoping to find a global optimum, and do not reconsider past choices. Dynamic Programming systematically explores all choices, reusing overlapping subproblems. Use Greedy when the problem has the 'greedy choice property', but use DP when future choices depend on overlapping past states.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "What is memoization vs tabulation in dynamic programming?", a: "Memoization is a top-down approach that uses recursion and caches the results of subproblems to avoid redundant calculations. Tabulation is a bottom-up approach that iteratively fills a table (like an array) starting from the base cases up to the final solution. Tabulation generally avoids recursion overhead and stack overflows.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "Dijkstra vs Bellman-Ford vs Floyd-Warshall — when to use each?", a: "Use Dijkstra for single-source shortest path on graphs with non-negative weights (fastest). Use Bellman-Ford for single-source paths when negative weights exist, or to detect negative cycles. Use Floyd-Warshall for all-pairs shortest paths on small, dense graphs.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is the difference between BFS and DFS? When to prefer which?", a: "BFS explores level by level using a Queue, making it ideal for finding the shortest path in unweighted graphs. DFS explores as deeply as possible using a Stack (or recursion), useful for topological sorting, cycle detection, or exploring all combinations (backtracking). BFS consumes more memory for wide trees, while DFS can get stuck in deep paths.", difficulty: "Intermediate" }
