          { q: "What is concurrency vs parallelism?", a: "Concurrency is about dealing with multiple tasks at once by overlapping their execution (often through context switching), providing the illusion of simultaneous execution. Parallelism is doing multiple tasks truly simultaneously using multiple CPU cores. Concurrency focuses on structure, while parallelism focuses on execution.", difficulty: "Basic" },
          { q: "What is a race condition and how to prevent it?", a: "A race condition occurs when multiple threads access shared data concurrently, and the final outcome depends on the unpredictable timing of their execution. It is prevented by using synchronization primitives like mutexes, locks, atomic variables, or thread-safe data structures to ensure mutually exclusive access.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "What are locks, mutexes, and semaphores? How do they differ?", a: "A lock or mutex provides mutual exclusion, allowing only one thread to access a resource at a time (ownership concept). A semaphore maintains a counter to control access to a pool of resources, allowing N threads to access it concurrently. A mutex is essentially a binary semaphore with ownership.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "What is a deadlock? What are the four necessary conditions?", a: "A deadlock is a state where threads are perpetually blocked, waiting for each other to release resources. The four Coffman conditions are: Mutual Exclusion, Hold and Wait, No Preemption, and Circular Wait. Breaking any of these conditions prevents deadlocks.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is a thread pool and why use one?", a: "A thread pool maintains a collection of pre-instantiated, reusable threads to execute tasks. It avoids the overhead of creating and destroying threads for every task. It also controls system resources by capping the maximum number of active threads, preventing exhaustion.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "What is the producer-consumer problem?", a: "It is a classic synchronization problem where producer threads generate data into a shared buffer, and consumer threads remove data from it. Synchronization is required to ensure producers don't add to a full buffer and consumers don't read from an empty one, typically solved using semaphores or condition variables.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "What is the dining philosophers problem?", a: "A classic concurrency problem illustrating deadlocks and resource allocation. Philosophers sit at a table needing two forks to eat. If everyone grabs their left fork simultaneously, they all wait forever for the right fork (deadlock). Solutions involve resource hierarchy or arbiters.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What are atomic operations?", a: "Atomic operations appear to execute as a single, indivisible step from the perspective of other threads. They complete fully or not at all, with no intermediate states visible. They are supported by hardware instructions (like Compare-And-Swap) and are fundamental for lock-free programming.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is optimistic locking vs pessimistic locking?", a: "Pessimistic locking locks the record before reading and holds it until the update finishes, preventing conflicts but reducing concurrency. Optimistic locking assumes no conflict will occur, reading data without locks and checking a version token upon saving. If the token changed, the transaction fails and must be retried.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is the difference between blocking and non-blocking I/O?", a: "Blocking I/O causes the calling thread to pause execution and wait until the I/O operation (like network or disk read) completes. Non-blocking I/O initiates the operation and returns immediately; the application is notified later via callbacks, events, or polling when the data is ready, allowing the thread to do other work.", difficulty: "Intermediate" }
