          { q: "What is Domain-Driven Design (DDD)?", a: "DDD is an approach to software development that centers on programming a domain model that has a deep understanding of the processes and rules of a domain. It emphasizes a ubiquitous language shared by developers and domain experts, and patterns like Bounded Contexts, Entities, Value Objects, and Aggregates.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is Event Sourcing?", a: "Instead of storing the current state of data, Event Sourcing stores a sequence of state-changing events. The current state is derived by replaying these events. It provides a complete audit trail, allows point-in-time state recovery, and aligns well with event-driven architectures.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)?", a: "CQRS separates the data modification operations (Commands) from data reading operations (Queries). This allows the read and write models to be optimized, scaled, and deployed independently. It is frequently paired with Event Sourcing and asynchronous read-model projections.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is the Strangler Fig pattern for migrating monoliths?", a: "It is a strategy for migrating a legacy monolithic application to a microservices architecture gradually. New features are built as modern services, and older parts are incrementally replaced over time. A proxy routes requests to either the legacy system or the new services until the monolith is entirely 'strangled'.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "Coupling vs Cohesion — what is good design?", a: "Good software design aims for low coupling and high cohesion. High cohesion means components within a module are closely related and focused on a single task. Low coupling means modules are independent and interact through well-defined interfaces, making changes localized and safer.", difficulty: "Basic" },
          { q: "What is technical debt and how to manage it?", a: "Technical debt is the implied cost of additional rework caused by choosing an easy, limited solution now instead of a better, longer-term approach. It is managed by tracking it in the backlog, dedicating specific sprints to refactoring, maintaining test coverage, and aligning code improvements with business goals.", difficulty: "Basic" }
