          { q: "How does a hash table work internally? What is load factor and rehashing?", a: "A hash table uses a hash function to compute an index into an array of buckets to store values. The load factor is the ratio of entries to buckets. When the load factor exceeds a threshold, the table undergoes rehashing: creating a larger array and recalculating bucket indices for all existing elements to maintain O(1) operations.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "How does a B-Tree / B+ Tree work? Why are they used in databases?", a: "B-Trees are self-balancing search trees with nodes containing multiple keys and children, optimized for disk reads by matching node size to disk blocks. B+ Trees store all values in the leaf nodes linked as a list, accelerating range queries. They drastically reduce disk I/O compared to binary trees.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is a bloom filter and when to use it?", a: "A bloom filter is a space-efficient probabilistic data structure used to test if an element is a member of a set. It can return false positives but never false negatives. It is highly effective for caching and databases to quickly check if a heavy disk lookup is necessary.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is a skip list?", a: "A skip list is a probabilistic alternative to balanced trees, consisting of linked lists in multiple layers. Higher layers act as 'express lanes' skipping over elements. It provides O(log n) average time complexity for search, insertion, and deletion while being significantly easier to implement concurrently.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is an LRU cache? How to implement it?", a: "An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache discards the least recently accessed items first when it reaches capacity. It is implemented using a HashMap and a Doubly Linked List. The Map provides O(1) lookups, while the list enables O(1) eviction and moving accessed items to the 'most recent' head.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "Difference between ArrayList and LinkedList in terms of performance", a: "ArrayLists are backed by arrays, providing O(1) random access but O(n) insertions/deletions in the middle due to shifting. LinkedLists use node pointers, offering O(1) insertions/deletions at known positions but O(n) access time. ArrayLists are far more cache-friendly for CPUs.", difficulty: "Basic" },
          { q: "What is a priority queue and how is it implemented (binary heap)?", a: "A priority queue returns elements based on priority rather than FIFO order. It is typically implemented via a Binary Heap array. In a max-heap, parent nodes are greater than children. Insertions and extractions take O(log n) time via up-heap (sift-up) and down-heap (sift-down) operations.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "What is a consistent hashing ring?", a: "Consistent hashing distributes data across servers using a hash ring. Both servers and data keys are hashed onto the ring. A key is assigned to the first server encountered clockwise. When servers are added or removed, only a small fraction (K/N) of keys are remapped, providing high scalability.", difficulty: "Advanced" }
