        questions: [
          { q: "What is service discovery? (Client-side vs Server-side)", a: "In microservices, IP addresses change dynamically. Service discovery acts as a registry (e.g., Consul, Eureka). In client-side, the client queries the registry and load-balances requests itself. In server-side, a load balancer queries the registry and forwards the traffic. It enables dynamic scaling.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "What is an API Gateway pattern?", a: "It provides a unified entry point for external clients, routing requests to appropriate internal microservices. It aggregates responses from multiple services and handles cross-cutting concerns like authentication, SSL, and rate limiting, hiding internal architectural complexity.", difficulty: "Basic" },
          { q: "What is the Sidecar pattern?", a: "The sidecar pattern deploys a helper component (container) alongside a primary application container. It handles auxiliary tasks like logging, proxying (Service Mesh/Envoy), and configuration management, isolating these concerns from the core business logic.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "What is the Bulkhead pattern?", a: "Borrowed from shipbuilding, this pattern isolates critical resources (like connection pools or threads) for different services. If one service experiences a surge in latency and exhausts its pool, the failure doesn't cascade, keeping the rest of the application functional.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is event-driven architecture vs request-driven?", a: "Request-driven uses synchronous REST/gRPC calls, causing tight coupling and potential cascading failures. Event-driven uses async message brokers (Kafka/RabbitMQ) where services publish and subscribe to domain events. It promotes massive scalability and decoupling but complicates debugging.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "What is the outbox pattern?", a: "It safely publishes events to a message broker when a database transaction occurs. Instead of directly publishing, the service writes the event to an 'outbox' table within the same ACID transaction. A separate background process reliably polls the outbox and publishes the messages to ensure guaranteed delivery.", difficulty: "Advanced" },
          { q: "What is a dead letter queue and why is it important?", a: "A DLQ is a secondary message queue where messages that fail processing multiple times are sent. It prevents poison pill messages from indefinitely blocking the main queue. Engineers monitor the DLQ to debug payload issues and manually replay messages once fixed.", difficulty: "Intermediate" },
          { q: "Choreography vs Orchestration in microservices", a: "In Orchestration, a central controller directs services to execute tasks (like a conductor). It is easier to trace but creates a single point of failure. In Choreography, services react to events emitted by others independently (like dancers). It is highly decoupled but harder to track overall business flows.", difficulty: "Advanced" }
        ]
